message: "If you use this data or code, please cite it as below."
title: "Detection is not resistance: frontier prompt-injection receipts"
authors:
  - family-names: Moon
    given-names: Bentley
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-0053-1661"
    website: "https://bentleymoon.com"
type: dataset
license: MIT
version: 1.0.1
date-released: "2026-09-17"
repository-code: "https://github.com/BentleyMoon/frontier-repro"
url: "https://bentleymoon.com"
keywords:
  - AI safety
  - prompt injection
  - LLM evaluation
  - oversight
  - verification
abstract: >
  Preregistered Batch-API tests of prompt-injection resistance across three frontier
  models (Claude Opus 4.8, Sonnet 5, Haiku 4.5). Sonnet 5 detects a blatant injection on
  every audit probe while obeying it on 16 of 20 tasks (0.80, 95% CI 0.58-0.92). This is an
  independent replication of detection without resistance, a mode reported first in
  arXiv:2606.07808; it is not claimed as a discovery. The obey rate replicates across four
  independent same-prompt batches (0.738, 0.787, 0.825, 0.850; mean 0.800) but is
  contingent on the injection being the last text in the prompt: on a length ladder with
  wording held constant, trailing neutral text takes it to 0.263 at 100 characters and
  0.000 at 200, and the same position contrast moved nothing on two open-weight models.
  The contribution beyond replication is the presentation channel: the identical payload
  moves Sonnet 5 from 0.00 to 0.33 compliance only when it wears a System, tool-output or
  cited-spec costume (+0.307, 90% CI 0.226-0.390, n=14), while the same model resists a
  fabricated reasoning technique (powered null). A block-labeling defense drove observed
  compliance to zero at n=14 (one-sided 95% upper bound about 19%); applied to a reviewing
  rather than an acting model it degraded discrimination, so it is scoped to the actor
  channel. Raw completions ship with stdlib-only re-derivation scripts (reproduce.py,
  joint_table.py, recency.py) requiring no API key and no network.
